// Original listening guides for selected works (work page "Listening guide" sheet). workId -> text (\n\n paragraphs).
// Each guide: context, what to listen for (movement by movement), a closing thought. Original prose; facts checked.
import { GUIDES_MORE as MORE_1, HOOKS_MORE as HOOKS_1 } from './guides-more-1.js';
import { GUIDES_MORE as MORE_2, HOOKS_MORE as HOOKS_2 } from './guides-more-2.js';
import { GUIDES_MORE as MORE_3, HOOKS_MORE as HOOKS_3 } from './guides-more-3.js';
import { GUIDES_MORE as MORE_4, HOOKS_MORE as HOOKS_4 } from './guides-more-4.js';

const p = (...paras) => paras.join('\n\n');

export const GUIDES = {
  'beethoven-symphony-5': p(
    `Beethoven sketched his Fifth over several years and finished it in 1808, when his hearing was failing and Vienna lived in the shadow of Napoleon’s wars. It was first heard on 22 December 1808 at the Theater an der Wien, in a freezing four-hour concert that also introduced the Pastoral Symphony and the Fourth Piano Concerto. Early listeners were bewildered; within two years the writer E. T. A. Hoffmann was hailing it as the essence of Romantic music.`,
    `Listen first for how much grows from four notes — three short, one long. In the first movement the motif is everywhere, tossed between instruments and piled into towering climaxes, interrupted only once, when a solo oboe sings a brief, lonely cadenza. The second movement is a set of variations that alternates gentle song with brassy, martial outbursts. In the third, cellos and basses creep up from the depths before the horns hammer out the rhythm of the opening; the trio is a gruff fugato for the low strings. Then the music sinks into a hush — plucked strings, a soft timpani heartbeat — and a long crescendo bursts into the blazing C major of the finale, where piccolo, contrabassoon and trombones join a Beethoven symphony for the first time. Midway through the finale, just before its opening returns, the shadow of the scherzo creeps back, a memory that makes the victory feel earned.`,
    `The journey from C minor darkness to C major light became a model that Brahms, Tchaikovsky and Mahler would all answer. Familiarity can dull it; try listening as if you did not know how it ends.`,
  ),

  'beethoven-symphony-7': p(
    `Beethoven completed his Seventh Symphony in 1812 and conducted its premiere on 8 December 1813 in the hall of Vienna University, at a charity concert for soldiers wounded fighting Napoleon. The success was immediate: the second movement had to be repeated on the spot. Wagner later called the work “the apotheosis of the dance”, and rhythm — insistent, obsessive, joyful — is its engine from first bar to last.`,
    `The first movement opens with a long slow introduction, broad chords and climbing scales that seem to search for a direction. When the Vivace arrives, it rides on a skipping long–short–long rhythm that hardly lets go; listen for the horns whooping near the end. The famous Allegretto in A minor is built on a single walking pattern — long, short-short, long, long — that begins low in violas and cellos and swells into a solemn procession, with a sorrowing countermelody above it that many listeners never forget. The Presto is a whirlwind, twice halted by a broader, hymn-like trio over a note held high in the violins. The finale is pure exhilaration: stamping accents on the off-beats, swirling strings, and a coda in which the basses grind back and forth on two notes as the whole orchestra drives towards the last chords.`,
    `Few symphonies are so physical. Let your pulse follow it: the Seventh is the rare masterpiece you feel in your feet as much as in your head.`,
  ),

  'beethoven-symphony-9': p(
    `Beethoven’s last symphony was first performed at the Kärntnertortheater in Vienna on 7 May 1824. By then almost totally deaf, he stood beside the conductor indicating the tempos, and at the end, the story goes, the contralto Caroline Unger turned him round so that he could see the applause he could not hear. For decades he had wanted to set Schiller’s ode “An die Freude”; bringing voices into a symphony was his radical solution.`,
    `The opening is one of music’s great beginnings: bare fifths trembling out of nothing, falling fragments gathering into a thunderous D minor theme. The second movement is a scherzo of manic energy, its fugal theme punctuated by octave strokes on the timpani. The slow third movement alternates two serene melodies in variation, with a famous solo for the fourth horn. Then the finale bursts in with a dissonant fanfare. Cellos and basses, like a singer in recitative, reject snatches of each earlier movement until the woodwinds hint at something new — and the low strings quietly take up the Ode to Joy, which grows through the orchestra. When the fanfare returns, the baritone answers it with Beethoven’s own words, “O friends, not these sounds!”, and the chorus enters. Listen for the tenor’s march with triangle and cymbals, the awed “Seid umschlungen, Millionen!” with trombones, the double fugue that combines both themes, and the delirious final race.`,
    `A hymn to brotherhood, written by a man cut off from the world by deafness. Its melody is now the anthem of Europe — but heard whole, the journey matters as much as the arrival.`,
  ),

  'beethoven-violin-concerto': p(
    `Beethoven wrote his only completed violin concerto in 1806 for Franz Clement, leader of the orchestra at the Theater an der Wien, who gave the premiere there on 23 December that year — reportedly with almost no rehearsal, the score was finished so late. It made little impression at first. Its fortunes changed in 1844, when the twelve-year-old Joseph Joachim played it in London under Mendelssohn; since then it has stood as the summit of the classical violin concerto.`,
    `The first movement begins with four soft taps on the timpani, and that quiet knocking returns throughout — sometimes gently, sometimes as a strange repeated note slightly out of key in the violins. The orchestra lays out a long, spacious introduction before the soloist enters, climbing in broken octaves as if from far away, and then spends much of the movement ornamenting the themes rather than battling the orchestra: this is a lyrical concerto, not a showpiece. Beethoven left no cadenza for the violin; you may hear one by Fritz Kreisler or Joachim, or one adapted from Beethoven’s own for his piano version of the work, complete with timpani. The Larghetto is a set of hushed variations, the violin floating above muted strings. A sudden outburst leads straight into the Rondo, a hunting dance in 6/8 with a tender duet for violin and bassoon in its minor-key episode.`,
    `This is music of patience and warmth, long-breathed and unhurried. Let it take its time; the rewards accumulate.`,
  ),

  'brahms-symphony-4': p(
    `Brahms wrote his last symphony over the summers of 1884 and 1885 in the Styrian resort of Mürzzuschlag, and conducted its premiere in Meiningen on 25 October 1885. Friends who first heard it on two pianos were puzzled by its severity, but it soon became, for many, his greatest orchestral work: autumnal, tightly argued and ending, unusually for its time, in tragedy rather than triumph.`,
    `The opening theme seems to begin mid-sentence — a chain of falling and rising thirds sighing in the violins. Notice how naturally this simple pattern grows into a movement of mounting passion. The Andante opens with horns in an archaic-sounding mode before the clarinets take up the melody; later the cellos sing one of Brahms’s warmest themes. The third movement, Allegro giocoso, is boisterous, and the only place in his symphonies where a triangle joins in. The finale is a passacaglia: an eight-bar theme adapted from a Bach cantata, announced by winds and by trombones, which only now enter the symphony. Thirty variations and a coda follow on that theme. Listen for the quiet, heartbreaking flute solo near the middle, the soft trombone chorale that follows, and the return of the theme in full force that launches the final stretch, gathering into a coda of fierce energy.`,
    `Brahms’s strictest form becomes a vessel for his most intense feeling. It is music that never stops thinking, yet never sounds academic.`,
  ),

  'brahms-violin-concerto': p(
    `Brahms wrote his Violin Concerto in the summer of 1878 at Pörtschach on the Wörthersee for his friend Joseph Joachim, the foremost violinist of the day, who advised him on the solo part. Joachim gave the premiere in Leipzig on New Year’s Day 1879, with Brahms conducting, and wrote the cadenza most violinists still play. Brahms had planned four movements; the two middle ones were replaced, as he wryly put it, by “a feeble Adagio”.`,
    `The first movement is vast and symphonic. A long orchestral introduction moves from calm, pastoral melody to stormy energy before the soloist bursts in with fiery double stops, as if forcing a way into the conversation. From then on violin and orchestra are partners more than rivals; the moment after the cadenza, when the soloist floats the main theme softly above the orchestra, is pure magic. The Adagio opens with one of the great oboe solos — a long, tender melody that the violin then embroiders and develops. (The virtuoso Pablo de Sarasate is said to have refused the piece because he did not want to stand idle while the oboe played the only tune.) The finale is a vigorous rondo with a Hungarian accent, a tribute to Joachim’s homeland, full of double-stopped swagger, and ends in a jaunty march.`,
    `Brahms was a pianist, yet he wrote one of the most violinistic concertos in the repertoire. Listen to it as a dialogue between two old friends.`,
  ),

  'mozart-symphony-41': p(
    `In the summer of 1788 Mozart wrote three symphonies in about six weeks — Nos. 39, 40 and 41 — with no known commission or occasion. The last, dated 10 August, was his final symphony. The nickname “Jupiter” came later and was not Mozart’s, but it suits the music’s brilliance and grandeur, with trumpets and timpani lighting up its festive C major.`,
    `The first movement sets martial outbursts against gentle, sighing replies, and near the end of the exposition slips into a cheerful little tune Mozart borrowed from a comic aria he had just written, “Un bacio di mano”. The Andante cantabile, with muted violins, is a meditation that turns unexpectedly troubled before calm returns. The Menuetto glides with chromatic grace; listen in its trio for a four-note figure that sounds like a closing cadence — it will return as the seed of the finale. The finale opens with four long notes, C–D–F–E, a motif of plainchant simplicity, and treats it with dazzling invention in a sonata form packed with counterpoint. The coda is the miracle: five themes from the movement are combined at once, each passing through the orchestra, while the music sounds as effortless as a dance.`,
    `We do not know whether Mozart ever heard this symphony performed. It stands as a summary of the Classical style — learned and joyful at once.`,
  ),

  'mozart-requiem': p(
    `In the summer of 1791 Mozart received, through an intermediary, an anonymous commission for a Requiem. The patron was Count Franz von Walsegg, who wished to honour his late wife — and, it seems, to pass the work off as his own. Busy with The Magic Flute and La clemenza di Tito, Mozart made real progress only in the autumn, and when he died on 5 December the work was unfinished. His widow Constanze, needing the fee, had it completed; the version usually performed is by his pupil Franz Xaver Süssmayr.`,
    `Only the opening Introit survives fully scored by Mozart. For the Kyrie, the Sequence and the Offertory he left the vocal parts, the bass line and instrumental hints. Listen to the dark colours he chose: basset horns and bassoons weave the opening, there are no flutes or oboes, and trombones add solemnity. The Kyrie is a fierce double fugue. The Dies irae erupts in terror; in the Tuba mirum a solo trombone answers the bass. Rex tremendae sets mighty cries of “Rex!” against a pleading close, and in the Confutatis the men’s voices, over snarling strings, alternate with the women’s prayerful “voca me”. Then the Lacrimosa rises in sighing steps; Mozart’s draft breaks off after eight bars. The Sanctus, Benedictus and Agnus Dei are largely Süssmayr’s, and the final Communion reuses Mozart’s opening music, so the work ends where it began.`,
    `Legends of poison and a grey-clad messenger grew around this score. The music needs none of them: it consoles as much as it frightens.`,
  ),

  'mozart-piano-concerto-21': p(
    `Mozart completed this concerto on 9 March 1785 and played it the next day at the Burgtheater in Vienna, in a season of concerts that showed him at the height of his popularity as a pianist-composer. He was writing concertos for his own fingers at an astonishing rate: this one followed the stormy D minor Concerto, K. 466, by barely a month.`,
    `The first movement opens with a quiet, march-like theme in the strings, answered by the winds. Listen to how Mozart’s orchestra is a partner rather than a backdrop — oboes, bassoons and flute keep up a running conversation with the soloist. The piano enters almost shyly, with a trill, before taking command, and the music makes surprising swerves into minor keys. Mozart’s own cadenzas are lost, so pianists write or choose their own. The Andante is the movement everyone knows: muted violins play a pulsing triplet accompaniment over plucked basses, and the piano sings a long, floating melody full of unexpected dissonances that resolve with infinite tenderness. It became famous after the 1967 Swedish film Elvira Madigan, a nickname the concerto still carries. The finale, Allegro vivace assai, is a quick, playful rondo of wit and rapid exchanges between piano and winds.`,
    `Mozart often finished these works days before performing them. They sound like a brilliant improviser trading ideas with friends on stage — which, in a sense, is exactly what they were.`,
  ),

  'tchaikovsky-violin-concerto': p(
    `In March 1878 Tchaikovsky was recovering at Clarens, on the shore of Lake Geneva, from the collapse of his brief marriage. When the young violinist Iosif Kotek joined him there, playing through music together rekindled his energy, and he wrote the whole concerto in less than a month. He replaced the first slow movement with a new one; the discarded piece survives as the “Méditation” for violin and piano. The intended dedicatee, Leopold Auer, declined to play it, and Adolph Brodsky finally gave the premiere in Vienna in December 1881, when the critic Eduard Hanslick savaged it.`,
    `After a short orchestral introduction, the violin enters with a brief improvised-sounding passage and then the main theme, warm and singing, which grows ever more ornate and virtuosic. The full orchestra later turns it into a grand polonaise. The cadenza comes not at the end but in the middle of the movement, before the recapitulation, and as it fades a solo flute gently takes up the main theme while the soloist accompanies. The second movement, a Canzonetta in G minor, is a melancholy song for muted violin over soft woodwind chords, and it runs without a break into the finale — a fiery Russian dance over drone basses, with folk-like tunes and gently rocking episodes before the race to the finish.`,
    `Hanslick’s review is remembered only because the concerto proved him so wrong. Today it is one of the most loved — and most demanding — works for the instrument, and it was born beside a Swiss lake.`,
  ),

  'tchaikovsky-pathetique': p(
    `Tchaikovsky conducted the premiere of his Sixth Symphony in St Petersburg on 28 October 1893 and died nine days later, officially of cholera. The coincidence has fed endless theories, but the symphony needs none: he considered it the best thing he had written and dedicated it to his nephew Vladimir Davydov. The title “Pathétique” — in Russian closer to “passionate” or “full of feeling” — came, so his brother Modest later claimed, from Modest himself.`,
    `The symphony begins in near darkness, a lone bassoon rising over divided double basses. The first movement’s fast music is restless, but its second theme, in D major, is one of Tchaikovsky’s most beloved melodies; listen for the moment when it dies away to almost nothing — he marks it pppppp — and a sudden crash shatters the calm. The second movement is a waltz in 5/4 time, an elegant dance that limps gracefully with one beat too many. The third begins as a scherzo and becomes a grand, overwhelming march; audiences often applaud after its thunderous ending, believing the symphony is over. It isn’t. The finale, Adagio lamentoso, is slow and grief-stricken: a falling melody split between first and second violins, a single soft stroke of the tam-tam, and a final descent into silence in the low strings.`,
    `Ending a symphony with a slow movement was almost unheard of. Hold the silence at the end — it is part of the music.`,
  ),

  'dvorak-new-world': p(
    `Dvořák wrote his Ninth Symphony in New York in 1893, during his first year as director of the National Conservatory of Music. He had listened closely to African American spirituals, sung to him by his student Harry T. Burleigh, and to Native American melodies, and argued that America’s music should grow from such sources. Yet the symphony quotes no borrowed tunes: its melodies are Dvořák’s own, coloured by the New World and by his homesickness for Bohemia. The premiere at Carnegie Hall on 16 December 1893 was a triumph.`,
    `After a slow introduction, the horns announce a striking, syncopated main theme; later a solo flute plays a melody with the lilt of a spiritual. The Largo is the heart of the work. After hushed brass chords, the cor anglais sings one of the most famous melodies ever written, later turned into the song “Goin’ Home” by one of Dvořák’s pupils. Listen for the brighter middle section with its woodwind birdcalls, and for the moment the melody returns, broken into fragments by the strings. The Scherzo was inspired, Dvořák said, by a dance scene in Longfellow’s The Song of Hiawatha, with Bohemian-sounding trios. The finale opens with a stern march-like theme for horns and trumpets, and along the way themes from all the earlier movements return in a grand summary. Listen too for a single, soft clash of the cymbal — its only appearance in the whole symphony.`,
    `It is a postcard home and a gift to his hosts at once. More than a century later it remains a symbol of music crossing oceans.`,
  ),

  'mahler-symphony-5': p(
    `Mahler wrote his Fifth Symphony in the summers of 1901 and 1902 in his composing hut at Maiernigg, on the Wörthersee. In between he met Alma Schindler and married her; the conductor Willem Mengelberg later reported that the Adagietto was a wordless love letter to her. Mahler conducted the premiere in Cologne in October 1904 and went on revising the orchestration almost to the end of his life. Unlike his earlier symphonies, it uses no voices and no programme.`,
    `The five movements fall into three parts. Part One opens with a solo trumpet fanfare that leads into a funeral march in C-sharp minor, torn by wild outbursts of grief; the second movement, marked “stormily, with the greatest vehemence”, revisits the same material in fevered struggle and glimpses a triumphant brass chorale before it collapses. Part Two is a vast Scherzo, a whirl of waltzes and country Ländler with a prominent solo horn. Part Three begins with the Adagietto, for strings and harp alone — slow, tender and intimate, and famous since Visconti used it in his 1971 film Death in Venice. The Rondo-Finale follows without a pause, a joyful movement full of counterpoint that even turns the Adagietto’s melody into a quick dance. At the end, the brass chorale glimpsed in the second movement finally returns in full glory.`,
    `The journey from funeral march to radiant chorale is less a story than a sequence of states of mind. Let yourself be carried along.`,
  ),

  'schubert-winterreise': p(
    `In 1827, a year before his death, Schubert set 24 poems by Wilhelm Müller, the poet of his earlier cycle Die schöne Müllerin. He found the first twelve in an almanac, then discovered that Müller had published twelve more, and set those too. When he sang the cycle to his friends they were stunned by its bleakness; Schubert said he liked these songs more than any others. He was correcting proofs of the second part in the last weeks of his life.`,
    `There is barely a plot: a young man, rejected in love, leaves town at night and wanders through a frozen landscape. The piano matters as much as the voice. In the first song, “Gute Nacht”, its steady walking rhythm sets out on the journey. In the fifth, “Der Lindenbaum”, rustling triplets and a horn-like tune evoke the linden tree under which he once dreamed; it later became a folk song in its own right. In “Die Post”, the thirteenth, the piano gallops like a post horse while he hopes in vain for a letter. Listen for “Die Krähe”, where a crow circles above him in the piano; “Der Wegweiser”, whose repeated notes trace a road from which no one returns; and the final song, “Der Leiermann”, an old hurdy-gurdy player grinding a drone in the snow. The wanderer asks whether he should go with him, and the question is never answered.`,
    `Winterreise is best heard in one sitting, in silence, without applause between songs. Its seventy-odd minutes pass like a single breath of cold air.`,
  ),

  'bach-goldberg': p(
    `Bach published this work in 1741 as the fourth part of his Clavier-Übung (“Keyboard Practice”), describing it as an aria with diverse variations for harpsichord with two manuals. The story goes that it was written for Count Keyserlingk, a Russian envoy who suffered from insomnia, and that his young harpsichordist Johann Gottlieb Goldberg played it to him at night. Historians doubt the tale, but the name has stuck.`,
    `The Aria is a gentle, ornamented sarabande. The thirty variations that follow are built not on its melody but on its bass line and harmonies, so the theme is felt rather than heard. Bach arranges them in groups of three: every third variation is a canon, in which one voice imitates another at a widening interval — first at the unison, then at the second, the third and so on up to the ninth. Between the canons come brilliant toccatas, often with hands crossing, and character pieces of every kind. Listen for Variation 16, a French overture that marks the halfway point; the three variations in G minor, above all the slow, chromatic Variation 25, which the harpsichordist Wanda Landowska called “the black pearl”; and Variation 30, a quodlibet in which Bach weaves popular songs of his day together, a joke shared among friends. Then the Aria returns, note for note — and sounds completely different after the journey.`,
    `On harpsichord or piano a performance can last from about forty to eighty minutes, depending on repeats. Glenn Gould’s two recordings, from 1955 and 1981, show how differently the same story can be told.`,
  ),

  'bach-st-matthew': p(
    `Bach’s St Matthew Passion was first heard in Leipzig’s Thomaskirche on Good Friday, 11 April 1727, and revised in 1736. Its text combines Matthew’s Gospel, sung by a tenor Evangelist, with reflective poems by Picander and Lutheran chorales the congregation knew by heart. After Bach’s death it fell silent until the twenty-year-old Mendelssohn revived it in Berlin in 1829 — a turning point in the rediscovery of Bach.`,
    `Bach divides his forces into two choirs and two orchestras that often answer one another. The opening chorus is overwhelming: over a rocking bass, the two choirs call and reply — “Come, you daughters, help me lament” — while a separate group of sopranos sings a chorale melody high above. Listen for the “halo” of sustained strings that surrounds the words of Jesus, and for the moment it vanishes, when he cries out on the cross, “My God, why hast thou forsaken me?”. The chorale “O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden” returns several times, with fresh harmonies each time. After Peter’s denial comes the alto aria “Erbarme dich”, with solo violin, one of the most moving pieces Bach wrote. The crowd’s cries of “Barrabam!” and “Let him be crucified!” are startlingly dramatic. The work closes with a vast, gentle lullaby at the tomb, “Wir setzen uns mit Tränen nieder”.`,
    `At nearly three hours, the Passion is less a concert than an experience. Many listeners return to it every Easter.`,
  ),

  'bach-christmas-oratorio': p(
    `Bach’s Christmas Oratorio is really six cantatas, first performed in Leipzig’s two main churches over the holidays of 1734–35, from Christmas Day to Epiphany. Much of the music was recycled from secular cantatas Bach had written to honour the Saxon royal family; the opening chorus, for instance, first celebrated the birthday of the Electress and Queen. With new words it became one of the most jubilant openings in sacred music.`,
    `Part I begins with a solo for timpani, answered by trumpets, flutes and oboes, before the choir bursts in with “Jauchzet, frohlocket!” — “Shout for joy, exult!”. The tenor Evangelist tells the story in recitative, while arias and chorales reflect on it; listen for the alto aria “Bereite dich, Zion”. Part II, for the shepherds, opens with a pastoral Sinfonia in which strings and flutes alternate with oboes d’amore and oboes da caccia, the shepherds’ pipes. Part III returns to festive trumpets. Part IV, for New Year’s Day, brings horns and a charming echo aria, in which a second soprano answers the first. Part V follows the Magi, and Part VI, for Epiphany, calls back the trumpets for a triumphant close. Its final chorale uses the melody heard in Part I and set so mournfully in the St Matthew Passion — now transformed into a song of victory.`,
    `Many ensembles perform Parts I–III as a single evening. The complete work, about two and a half hours, is well worth the time.`,
  ),

  'vivaldi-four-seasons': p(
    `The Four Seasons are the first four of twelve concertos Vivaldi published in Amsterdam in 1725 under the title Il cimento dell’armonia e dell’inventione — “The contest between harmony and invention”. Each is paired with a sonnet, possibly by Vivaldi himself, and lines from the poems are printed in the score at the very passages they describe. Seldom has music told stories so vividly.`,
    `In Spring, the birds greet the season with trills and chirps from the solo violin and its neighbours in the orchestra, brooks murmur, and a short thunderstorm passes. In the slow movement a goatherd sleeps while the violas imitate his barking dog, a rough, repeated two-note figure; a rustic dance closes the concerto. Summer is heavy with heat: listen for the cuckoo, the turtle dove and the goldfinch, and for the storm that breaks in the finale with furious scales. Autumn begins with peasants celebrating the harvest, some of them drunk — note the staggering violin lines — dozes through a hazy, muted slow movement, then sets out on a hunt with horn-like calls. Winter is the most astonishing: teeth chatter in icy repeated notes; the slow movement sits by the fire while rain falls outside, painted by plucked violins; and the finale shows people walking carefully on ice, slipping and falling.`,
    `Heard so often, these concertos can fade into background music. Read the sonnets as you listen and they come alive again as some of the earliest programme music ever written.`,
  ),

  'ravel-bolero': p(
    `Boléro was commissioned by the dancer Ida Rubinstein and first staged at the Paris Opéra on 22 November 1928, choreographed by Bronislava Nijinska. Ravel considered it an experiment in a single, narrow direction — orchestration and crescendo, with almost nothing developed — and called it a piece for orchestra without music. It became one of the most famous works of the twentieth century, a success that slightly embarrassed him.`,
    `The recipe is simple. A snare drum quietly begins a rhythmic pattern that it repeats, without stopping, for some fifteen minutes. Over it, a long melody in two halves — the first calm and diatonic, the second more sinuous, with exotic inflections — passes from instrument to instrument: flute first, then clarinet, bassoon high in its register, the little E-flat clarinet, oboe d’amore, and on through the orchestra. Listen for the strange, organ-like sound when a horn, celesta and two piccolos play the tune in different keys at once, blending into one new colour; for the saxophones; and for the trombone solo with its sly slides. Little by little the orchestra fills in, louder and denser, but the music never leaves C major — until, near the very end, it lurches into E major, a jolt after so long in one key, before collapsing back and crashing to a dissonant halt.`,
    `It is a masterclass in orchestral colour and a lesson in patience. Next time, follow a single instrument from start to finish.`,
  ),

  'debussy-la-mer': p(
    `Debussy began La Mer in 1903 in Burgundy, far from any coast, and completed it in March 1905; that summer he corrected the proofs by the sea at Eastbourne, in England. He loved the sea — he claimed he had once been destined for a sailor’s life — and drew on memory and art as much as observation: the cover of the first edition reproduced Hokusai’s print The Great Wave off Kanagawa. The Paris premiere in October 1905 was coolly received, but La Mer soon became a cornerstone of the orchestral repertoire.`,
    `Debussy called the three movements “symphonic sketches”, and they flow more like weather than like classical forms. “From dawn to noon on the sea” begins in near darkness, with low murmurs and fragments, the light gradually breaking through; listen for the cellos, divided into several groups, rippling like waves, and for the brass chorale that crowns the movement at noon. “Play of the waves” is a sparkling scherzo of shifting colours, harps, glockenspiel and triangle glinting on the surface. “Dialogue of the wind and the sea” is stormier: rumbling low strings and timpani, a plaintive woodwind theme, and the return of the chorale, now blazing as the full orchestra reaches a thrilling climax.`,
    `Don’t try to see pictures; let the colours wash over you. Debussy wanted to capture what the sea feels like, not to paint postcards.`,
  ),

  'stravinsky-rite': p(
    `The Rite of Spring was largely composed at Clarens, on Lake Geneva, where Stravinsky worked in a small room with little more than an upright piano, finishing the composition in late 1912. Written for Diaghilev’s Ballets Russes, with choreography by Vaslav Nijinsky and designs by Nicholas Roerich, it presents “pictures of pagan Russia” in which a young girl dances herself to death. The premiere at the Théâtre des Champs-Élysées in Paris on 29 May 1913, conducted by Pierre Monteux, provoked one of the most famous uproars in music history.`,
    `The opening is unforgettable: a solo bassoon, strained high in its register, plays a Lithuanian folk tune, and one by one other woodwinds join until the music becomes a tangle of awakening nature. Then the strings pound out a single dissonant chord with unpredictable accents — the “Augurs of Spring” — that seems to stamp the ground. Throughout Part I, “The Adoration of the Earth”, listen for brutal rhythms and folk melodies piled on top of each other. Part II, “The Sacrifice”, opens in mysterious calm. Eleven pounding blows announce the choice of the victim, and the music grows ever more violent. The final “Sacrificial Dance” is a convulsion of constantly changing metres, until a last rush and a brutal chord bring it to an end.`,
    `More than a century later, The Rite still sounds raw and new. Stravinsky said he was merely the vessel through which it passed; listen to it as a force of nature.`,
  ),

  'rachmaninoff-piano-concerto-2': p(
    `After the disastrous premiere of his First Symphony in 1897, Rachmaninoff fell into a depression and composed almost nothing for three years. Sessions with the physician Nikolai Dahl, who used hypnotherapy, helped him regain his confidence, and he dedicated his Second Piano Concerto to him. He played the solo part at the first complete performance in Moscow in November 1901, with his cousin Alexander Siloti conducting, and the concerto has been a favourite ever since.`,
    `The opening is famous: the piano alone plays a series of chords, swelling like tolling bells, until the orchestra launches the broad, dark main theme in the strings while the piano churns beneath in surging arpeggios. The second theme, given to the piano, is tender and lyrical. In the recapitulation the main theme returns as a powerful march. The slow movement, Adagio sostenuto, begins with muted strings; the piano sets up gentle rocking figures before a flute and then a clarinet sing the main melody — one of the most beautiful passages Rachmaninoff ever wrote. The finale opens with a nervous, scherzo-like energy; then oboe and violas introduce a broad, sweeping melody, later taken up by the piano, which returns at the end in a triumphant tutti.`,
    `Its melodies have drifted into films and popular songs, notably David Lean’s Brief Encounter. Nothing, though, replaces those opening chords heard live in a concert hall.`,
  ),

  'puccini-tosca': p(
    `Puccini based Tosca on a play by Victorien Sardou that Sarah Bernhardt had made famous, with a libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa. The first night was at Rome’s Teatro Costanzi on 14 January 1900. It is set in that same city on 17 and 18 June 1800, as news arrives of Napoleon’s battle at Marengo, and each act takes place in a real Roman landmark.`,
    `Act I is set in the church of Sant’Andrea della Valle, where the painter Cavaradossi hides an escaped political prisoner. Listen to the three crashing chords at the very beginning: they belong to Scarpia, the sinister chief of police. Cavaradossi sings “Recondita armonia”, comparing the beauty of the singer Floria Tosca with the portrait he is painting; the act ends with a grand Te Deum — cannon, bells and chorus — while Scarpia gloats over his desire for Tosca. Act II takes place in Scarpia’s apartment in the Palazzo Farnese. While her lover is tortured offstage, Tosca sings her prayer “Vissi d’arte”, then stabs Scarpia with a knife from his own supper table. Act III opens at dawn on the ramparts of Castel Sant’Angelo, with a shepherd boy singing and the bells of Rome ringing — Puccini researched their exact pitches. A solo clarinet introduces Cavaradossi’s farewell, “E lucevan le stelle”. The ending is swift and tragic.`,
    `The critic Joseph Kerman dismissed it as a “shabby little shocker”, but its theatrical power is undeniable. Few operas move with such ruthless efficiency.`,
  ),

  'bizet-carmen': p(
    `Carmen premiered at the Opéra-Comique in Paris on 3 March 1875. The libretto, by Henri Meilhac and Ludovic Halévy, was based on a novella by Prosper Mérimée. The story of a free-spirited Roma woman who seduces a soldier, Don José, and then leaves him for a bullfighter shocked part of its audience, and the reception was mixed. Bizet died three months later, believing it a failure. It is now one of the most performed operas in the world.`,
    `The Prelude begins with the brilliant music of the bullring and the Toreador’s tune, then darkens with the “fate” motif. In Act I, in a square in Seville, Carmen sings the Habanera, “L’amour est un oiseau rebelle”, adapted from a song by the Spanish composer Sebastián Yradier that Bizet took to be a folk tune; later she ensnares José with the Seguidilla. Act II opens in Lillas Pastia’s tavern with a whirling gypsy dance. The bullfighter Escamillo makes his entrance with the famous Toreador Song, and Don José answers with his passionate Flower Song. In Act III, at the smugglers’ camp in the mountains, Carmen reads her death in the cards, and Micaëla, the girl from José’s village, sings a touching prayer. Act IV takes place outside the bullring. While the crowd inside cheers Escamillo, the final confrontation between Carmen and José plays out.`,
    `Carmen was first an opéra comique with spoken dialogue; after Bizet’s death Ernest Guiraud set the dialogue as sung recitative. You may hear either version — both overflow with unforgettable tunes.`,
  ),

  'honegger-pacific-231': p(
    `Arthur Honegger had a passion for steam engines — “I have always loved locomotives passionately,” he wrote, and he saw them almost as living creatures. Pacific 231, completed in 1923, takes its name from a class of heavy express engine with a 2-3-1 wheel arrangement. First performed at the Paris Opéra in May 1924 under Serge Koussevitzky, and dedicated to the Swiss conductor Ernest Ansermet, it was an instant sensation and became an icon of the machine age.`,
    `Yet Honegger insisted he was not imitating the noise of a train. He wanted to capture what a speeding engine looks like and the bodily thrill of speed, and he called the piece an abstract experiment: rhythm quickening with mathematical regularity while the underlying motion grows slower. At the start you hear the engine at rest: soft, hissing sounds in the strings and a few heavy breaths. Then it begins to move, slowly at first. Listen to how the note values grow shorter and shorter, giving the sense of gathering speed even as the underlying beat broadens. At full speed a powerful theme emerges in the brass, carried along by the whole orchestra like the train itself thundering through the night. Then the brakes take hold, the rhythm unwinds, and the work ends with a massive final chord.`,
    `In barely seven minutes, a Swiss composer born in Le Havre captured the thrill of modern technology. It remains a dazzling showpiece for any orchestra.`,
  ),

  'rstrauss-four-last-songs': p(
    `In 1948, at the age of 84, Richard Strauss was living in Switzerland. He composed “Im Abendrot”, a setting of Joseph von Eichendorff, in Montreux that spring; over the summer, partly in Pontresina in the Engadine, he set three poems by Hermann Hesse — “Frühling”, “Beim Schlafengehen” and “September”. He did not conceive them as a cycle and never heard them performed. His publisher Ernst Roth gave them their order and title, and Kirsten Flagstad sang the premiere in London in May 1950, with Wilhelm Furtwängler conducting.`,
    `In “Frühling” (Spring) the soprano soars above a shimmering orchestra, as if the season were a vision from long ago. In “September”, summer fades gently, and the song ends with a long, serene horn solo — Strauss’s father had been a horn player. In “Beim Schlafengehen” (Going to sleep) the soul longs to fly free into the night; listen for the solo violin that rises before the final verse, as if lending it wings. “Im Abendrot” (At sunset) describes an old couple who have walked hand in hand through life. Two flutes trill like larks climbing into the evening sky, and when the soprano asks, “Is this perhaps death?”, the orchestra softly recalls a theme from Strauss’s tone poem Death and Transfiguration, written sixty years earlier.`,
    `This is Strauss’s farewell: an act of acceptance and peace, and a last love letter to the soprano voice he had cherished all his life.`,
  ),

  'handel-messiah': p(
    `Handel composed Messiah in about three and a half weeks in the summer of 1741, setting a text that Charles Jennens had assembled from the Bible. Dublin heard it first, on 13 April 1742, at a charity concert in the new Music Hall on Fishamble Street; ladies were asked not to wear hooped skirts so that more people could fit in. After a cooler reception in London, annual performances for the Foundling Hospital made it the best-loved oratorio in the English-speaking world.`,
    `Messiah is unusual among Handel’s oratorios: there are no characters, only a meditation in three parts. Part One covers prophecy and the birth of Christ. After a French-style overture, a tenor sings the consoling “Comfort ye” and the bright aria “Every valley”. Listen for the chorus “For unto us a child is born”, whose light, rapid runs burst into the cry “Wonderful, Counsellor”; the short Pastoral Symphony evoking the shepherds; and the angels’ “Glory to God”, where trumpets are heard for the first time. Part Two turns to the Passion. The alto aria “He was despised” is the work’s emotional heart, and the section ends with the “Hallelujah” chorus, for which audiences traditionally stand. Part Three, on the resurrection, includes the soprano aria “I know that my Redeemer liveth”, the bass aria “The trumpet shall sound” with its trumpet solo, and the final chorus “Worthy is the Lamb”, crowned by a long fugal Amen.`,
    `Many choirs sing Messiah at Christmas, but it was first heard in April, in the days just before Easter. It is a work for any season.`,
  ),

  'mendelssohn-violin-concerto': p(
    `Mendelssohn wrote his Violin Concerto for his friend Ferdinand David, concertmaster of the Leipzig Gewandhaus Orchestra, who advised him on the solo part. The idea took six years to ripen; the score was finished in 1844, and David gave the premiere in Leipzig on 13 March 1845, with Niels Gade conducting because Mendelssohn was unwell. It quickly became one of the most popular violin concertos ever written.`,
    `Its innovations are everywhere. Instead of a long orchestral introduction, the soloist enters almost at once, singing the passionate main theme over murmuring strings. The cadenza, written out by Mendelssohn himself, comes before the recapitulation rather than at the end, and as it finishes the orchestra steals back in with the main theme while the soloist plays shimmering arpeggios above. Rather than stopping, a single bassoon holds a note that leads straight into the slow movement, a tender song without words. A short, wistful bridge leads to the finale, which bursts in with a fanfare and turns into a sparkling, elfin dance in the spirit of A Midsummer Night’s Dream, full of virtuoso fireworks.`,
    `The three movements are played without a break, so there is no pause for applause — and you will not want one. Few concertos are so perfectly formed.`,
  ),

  'bloch-schelomo': p(
    `Ernest Bloch wrote Schelomo in his native Geneva in 1915–16, shortly before leaving for America. He had been planning a vocal setting of Ecclesiastes, the book traditionally attributed to King Solomon, until he met the Russian cellist Alexandre Barjansky; the cello’s voice, he decided, could speak for Solomon better than any singer. A wax statuette of Solomon made by Barjansky’s wife, Catherine, completed the inspiration. The work was first performed at Carnegie Hall in New York in May 1917.`,
    `Bloch described the solo cello as the voice of Solomon and the orchestra as the world around him. The piece unfolds in a single movement in three broad sections. It begins with a long, improvisatory lament for the cello, full of sighing, melismatic phrases that recall Jewish cantillation, before the orchestra adds dark, exotic colours. The central section is more animated, with a march-like theme and a dance of oriental colour, rising to an orchestral climax of overwhelming splendour that seems to evoke Solomon’s wealth and power. Listen for how the cello keeps interrupting these displays with its brooding monologue. In the last section the opening lament returns, and the final pages are the most moving: the cello sinks to its lowest notes, as if pronouncing that all is vanity.`,
    `Schelomo is one of the great works for cello and orchestra of the twentieth century — the masterpiece of a Swiss composer who found his voice in the music of his ancestors.`,
  ),

  'martin-petite-symphonie': p(
    `In 1944 the Basel conductor Paul Sacher asked Frank Martin for a work featuring an unusual trio of soloists — harp, harpsichord and piano — with a double string orchestra. Martin accepted the challenge and finished the score in 1945, and Sacher conducted the premiere in Zurich on 17 May 1946 with the Collegium Musicum Zürich. It became Martin’s most popular work, and he later arranged it for full orchestra as the Symphonie concertante.`,
    `The challenge was to balance three plucked or struck instruments that differ so much in sound and volume. Martin keeps them distinct: the harp gentle and resonant, the harpsichord bright and incisive, the piano rich and powerful, while the two string orchestras answer and echo one another. The work is in two parts. The first opens with a slow, mysterious Adagio whose theme uses all twelve notes of the chromatic scale, yet sounds anything but dry, and leads into an energetic Allegro full of rhythmic drive. The second part begins with a calm, lyrical Adagio, then quickens into a sparkling march, Allegretto alla marcia, that gathers all the instruments into a joyful finale.`,
    `Its subtle, original colours make it one of the masterpieces of twentieth-century Swiss music, and the perfect introduction to Frank Martin’s language.`,
  ),
};

